# Line endings.
#
# There is no .editorconfig any more - the coding standard is
# Opinionated.DotNet.CodingStandards, a GlobalPackageReference in Directory.Packages.props -
# and its `end_of_line = crlf` governs editors only. This file governs git, which is the half
# that decides what actually lands in a commit. The two are complementary, not alternatives.
#
# Without this file, line endings are a property of each contributor's core.autocrlf rather
# than of the repository: a clone with autocrlf=false commits CRLF verbatim, and the next
# checkout elsewhere produces a whole-file diff against it. That is not hypothetical here -
# seven .csproj files in this tree already carry MIXED endings, which is what the problem
# looks like before anyone notices it.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository, whatever core.autocrlf is set to. This
# is the rule that actually closes the hole; everything below is refinement. Git keeps
# auto-detecting binaries under `auto`, which is why Yello.Client/wwwroot/favicon.png and
# _bmad/_config/bmad-help.csv - both of which git already reads as binary - are safe.
* text=auto

# The coding standard states end_of_line = crlf, so pin the WORKING TREE to CRLF for the file
# types it governs, on every platform rather than only where core.autocrlf happens to be true.
# The stored copy is still LF: `eol` affects checkout, not the repository.
#
# The concrete failure this prevents: CI (story 1.10) running on a Linux image would check out
# LF, then check that tree against a standard that says CRLF. `dotnet format` reads
# end_of_line, so the tree and the rule it is measured by must not disagree by platform.
#
# Note what is deliberately NOT listed: *.json, *.md, *.py, *.toml and the rest of the
# .claude/skills tree. Those are vendored tool output or documentation, no build gate reads
# their line endings, and `text=auto` above already gives them native endings per platform.
*.cs        text eol=crlf
*.razor     text eol=crlf
*.csproj    text eol=crlf
*.props     text eol=crlf
*.targets   text eol=crlf
*.slnx      text eol=crlf

# The inverse: files that break if they arrive with CRLF, whatever the host runs. None of
# these exist yet. CI arrives in story 1.10, and a shell script committed from Windows and
# then run in a Linux container is the textbook way this bites - `$'\r': command not found`,
# from a file that looks correct in every editor. Cheaper to state now than to debug later.
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.bash       text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Explicit rather than inferred. Git guesses both of these correctly today, but a guess is
# not a gate, and a corrupted binary is a worse failure than a noisy diff.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
